cartridge "ISLAND QUEST/B.BLACKMER/1988"

' ISLAND QUEST
' A ColecoVision game based on "Island Quest" by Bob Blackmer, Ahoy!
' November 1988 (Commodore 64 machine language, ISLAND QUEST.ML at $C000,
' started by a two-line BASIC loader). Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an
' AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' Not a line-by-line port: the rules are the original's (read from its
' machine code, orig/islandquest-ml.dis), the game is built for the
' ColecoVision: big 16 x 16 map cells (grass, palms, rocks, sea, sand
' mounds, a hut), a two-colour pirate sprite that walks, a sector map with
' a cursor sprite, treasure chests for the booty, sound effects.
'
' The game: you are stranded on a big island. Six treasures are buried
' under six of thirteen sand mounds, your home lies under another, six are
' plain dirt. Walk the island and dig. Shells hidden in the grass are
' clues: each one found gives the sector map square of the next treasure.
' Dig up all six treasures, find your home and stand on it with the booty
' to win, before the clock reaches 60 minutes.
'
' From the original (orig/): the island of 110 x 110 cells inside the sea
' (rows 8-119, columns 9-118; a row of trees along the top and bottom
' shores), three random grass kinds; a 10 x 5 grid of places (rows 18, 28
' ... 108, columns 18, 38 ... 98) of which 19 different ones are drawn at
' random: 13 sites (trees above, sea left and right, the mound in the
' middle, trees below: the first is home, the next six hold the treasures,
' the last six dirt) and 6 clue spots that look almost like grass; the
' player starts at row 9, column 9 and walks one cell at a time in eight
' directions (the C64 read up/down and left/right separately) inside rows
' and columns 9-118; a clue is found by stepping on it, the n-th clue tells
' where treasure n is; digging works only on a mound; with six treasures,
' the button on the found home wins; the clock counts up minutes and
' seconds and ends the game at 60:00; the messages (YOU FOUND TREASURE!,
' NOTHING BUT DIRT!, YOU FOUND A CLUE!, YOU FOUND YOUR HOME!, YOU'RE A
' WINNER, MATE!, YOU RAN OUT OF TIME!); the 10 x 10 sector map with rows
' 0-9 and columns A-J and its cursor moving one square every 12 steps.
'
' Liberties: the view shows 7 x 7 cells of 16 pixels (the C64 showed 9 x 7
' cells of 8 pixels in a small window); a clue now names the sector map
' square the treasure's mound is really in (the C64 computed the clue from
' the grid place with a different rounding, so a clue could point one
' square off its cursor); dug mounds are marked on the sector map; digging
' anywhere else gives a thud (the C64 did nothing); the messages stay on the
' bottom line while you walk on (the C64 waited for the trigger after each
' one); digging up home with the six treasures already found wins at once
' (the C64 wanted one more press); a step every 9 frames while the stick is
' held (the C64's two delay loops gave about 3.4 steps a second); the sea
' around the island is plain sea (the C64 put random grass in its corners);
' the best time is kept for the title; the game end message, then the
' title (the C64 waited for the trigger and built a new island).
'
' Controls: controller 1 (or 2): the stick walks, a button digs. On the
' title, a button starts.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` is always given. Own
' xorshift random numbers.

